IMPORT prelude

-- Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth) -- who is an employee (ss 13, 15AA, s 12 "vocational placement", "junior employee"), the employee-like
-- worker and what a minimum standards order may say (ss 15N, 15P, 536KL, 536KM, 536KMA), the National Employment Standards the
-- Western Australian lives ask about (flexible working requests ss 65, 65A; unpaid parental leave ss 67, 70, 71, 76, 84; annual
-- leave ss 87, 90; personal/carer's leave s 96; jury service s 111; public holidays ss 114-116; notice of termination ss 117,
-- 118; redundancy pay ss 119, 121, 123; small business employer s 23), unfair dismissal eligibility (ss 382, 383) and genuine
-- redundancy (s 389), notice to Centrelink (s 530), pay slips (s 536), and recovering an underpayment (ss 45, 544, 545, 548).
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 73, compilation date 7 July 2026 (includes amendments up to Act No. 62, 2026), register id
-- C2026C00355, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2009A00028.txt.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0002 in subjects/au/requirements.jsonl (priority 1), raised 22 times by the three Western
-- Australian cradle-to-grave scenarios (Chloe c020, c032, c040, c041, c042, c056, c061, c063, c067, c072, c077; Jake k040,
-- k041, k060, k061, k062, k086, k087; Daniel d030, d042, d044, d066). SCOPED to the provisions those events name, with the
-- definitions, conditions and exceptions needed to answer them.
--
-- NOT ENCODED. Modern awards themselves (the General Retail, Security Services and Cleaning Services Awards are not in the Act
-- and were not deposited: junior rates, overtime, shift penalties and minimum rates are INPUTS); the national minimum wage;
-- every figure set by regulation or indexation (the high income threshold, a prescribed small claims limit above $100,000,
-- jury service pay) -- these are INPUTS; the 15AA multi-factor test itself (whether someone is an employee in the ordinary
-- meaning is an INPUT); the 15AB opt-out; enterprise agreements; the shiftworker test in s 87(3); flexible unpaid parental
-- leave (s 72A) beyond noting it; keeping in touch days; s 121(4) (small business employer that becomes insolvent); s 120
-- variation by the FWC; s 122 transfer of employment; the superannuation contribution duty in Division 10A (s 116B) beyond
-- noting it; the unfair dismissal remedy; the general protections; civil penalties.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Fair Work Act 2009 -- employment`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the greater of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT LEAST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `ss 12, 13, 15AA -- who is a national system employee`

-- s 13: "A national system employee is an individual so far as he or she is employed, or usually employed ... by a national
-- system employer, except on a vocational placement."
-- s 12: "vocational placement means a placement that is: (a) undertaken with an employer for which a person is not entitled to be
-- paid any remuneration; and (b) undertaken as a requirement of an education or training course; and (c) authorised under a law
-- or an administrative arrangement of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory."
-- s 15AA(1): whether an individual is an employee "within the ordinary meaning" is determined "by ascertaining the real substance,
-- practical reality and true nature of the relationship"; (2) "the totality of the relationship ... must be considered", including
-- "how the contract is performed in practice". That is a multi-factor judgment: it is an INPUT here.
GIVEN `entitled to be paid` IS A BOOLEAN, `required by a course` IS A BOOLEAN, `authorised by law or arrangement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the placement is a vocational placement` IF
        NOT `entitled to be paid`
    AND `required by a course`
    AND `authorised by law or arrangement`

GIVEN `an employee in the ordinary meaning` IS A BOOLEAN, `employer is a national system employer` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `on a vocational placement` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the individual is a national system employee` IF
        `an employee in the ordinary meaning`
    AND `employer is a national system employer`
    AND (NOT `on a vocational placement`)

-- s 12: "junior employee means a national system employee who is under 21." s 139(1)(a): a modern award may include terms about
-- "minimum wages (including wage rates for junior employees ...)". The junior RATE is in the award, not the Act.
GIVEN `age in years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employee is a junior employee` IF
    `age in years` LESS THAN 21

§§ `ss 15N, 15P -- employee-like worker`

-- s 15P(1): a person is an employee-like worker if (a) an individual party to a services contract who performs work under it;
-- (b) "performs all, or a significant majority, of the work"; (c) "the work ... is digital platform work"; (d) "does not perform any
-- work under the services contract as an employee"; and (e) "satisfies 2 or more of" (i) "low bargaining power", (ii) "remuneration
-- at or below the rate of an employee performing comparable work", (iii) "a low degree of authority over the performance of the
-- work", (iv) prescribed characteristics. s 15N(1)(a): digital platform work is work by an independent contractor under a services
-- contract "through or by means of a digital labour platform" for which "payment is made".
DECLARE PlatformWorker HAS
    `individual party performing work under a services contract` IS A BOOLEAN
    `performs all or a significant majority of the work` IS A BOOLEAN
    `work is through a digital labour platform and paid` IS A BOOLEAN
    `works under the contract as an employee` IS A BOOLEAN
    `low bargaining power` IS A BOOLEAN
    `paid at or below a comparable employee` IS A BOOLEAN
    `low authority over the work` IS A BOOLEAN
    `has a prescribed characteristic` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `b` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`one if` MEANS IF `b` THEN 1 ELSE 0

GIVEN `w` IS A PlatformWorker
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the worker is an employee-like worker` IF
        `w`'s `individual party performing work under a services contract`
    AND `w`'s `performs all or a significant majority of the work`
    AND `w`'s `work is through a digital labour platform and paid`
    AND (NOT `w`'s `works under the contract as an employee`)
    AND     `one if` (`w`'s `low bargaining power`)
          PLUS `one if` (`w`'s `paid at or below a comparable employee`)
          PLUS `one if` (`w`'s `low authority over the work`)
          PLUS `one if` (`w`'s `has a prescribed characteristic`)
        AT LEAST 2

§§ `ss 536KL, 536KM, 536KMA -- what a minimum standards order may contain`

-- s 536KL(1): "A minimum standards order may include terms about any of the following matters: (a) payment terms; (b) deductions;
-- (d) record-keeping ...; (e) insurance; (f) consultation; (g) representation; (h) delegates' rights; (i) cost recovery." (There is
-- no paragraph (c) in the deposit.) (2): "The matters listed in subsection (1) do not limit the terms that may be included".
-- s 536KM(1): must NOT include terms about "(a) overtime rates; (b) rostering arrangements; ... (d) a term that would change the form
-- of the engagement or the status of regulated workers ... including ... a term that deems a regulated worker to be an employee".
-- s 536KMA(1): an employee-like worker order must not include terms about "(a) penalty rates ...", "(c) minimum periods of engagement
-- ..."; but (2) such a term "may be included ... if the FWC is satisfied that the inclusion of the term is appropriate".
-- Superannuation is neither listed in s 536KL(1) nor barred by s 536KM or s 536KMA. READING (an inference): because the s 536KL
-- list is non-limiting, a superannuation term is not excluded by the Act.
DECLARE OrderTerm IS ONE OF
    `payment terms`
    `deductions`
    `insurance`
    `superannuation`
    `overtime rates`
    `rostering arrangements`
    `a term deeming the worker an employee`
    `penalty rates`
    `minimum periods of engagement`

GIVEN `term` IS A OrderTerm, `FWC satisfied the term is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an employee-like worker order may include the term` IF
    CONSIDER `term`
    WHEN `overtime rates` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `rostering arrangements` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a term deeming the worker an employee` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `penalty rates` THEN `FWC satisfied the term is appropriate`
    WHEN `minimum periods of engagement` THEN `FWC satisfied the term is appropriate`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `ss 65, 65A -- requests for flexible working arrangements`

-- s 65(1A): circumstances include "(aa) the employee is pregnant; (a) the employee is the parent, or has responsibility for the care,
-- of a child who is of school age or younger; (b) ... a carer; (c) ... a disability; (d) ... 55 or older; (e) ... experiencing family
-- and domestic violence; (f) ... provides care or support to a member ... experiencing family and domestic violence."
-- (1B): a parent "returning to work after taking leave in relation to the birth or adoption of the child ... may request to work
-- part-time". (2) not entitled unless "(a) for an employee other than a casual employee -- ... at least 12 months of continuous service
-- ... immediately before making the request; or (b) for a casual employee -- ... a regular casual employee ... for ... at least 12
-- months; and ... a reasonable expectation of continuing employment". (3) the request must be in writing and give details and reasons.
-- s 22(4): for Division 4 of Part 2-2, only unauthorised absence (and prescribed periods) is left out of service, so unpaid parental
-- leave counts toward the 12 months.
GIVEN `a listed circumstance applies` IS A BOOLEAN, `casual` IS A BOOLEAN, `months of continuous service` IS A NUMBER,
      `regular casual for 12 months with an expectation of continuing` IS A BOOLEAN, `in writing with details and reasons` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employee may make a flexible working request` IF
        `a listed circumstance applies`
    AND `in writing with details and reasons`
    AND (    ((NOT `casual`) AND `months of continuous service` AT LEAST 12)
         OR  (`casual` AND `regular casual for 12 months with an expectation of continuing`))

-- s 65A(1): "a written response ... within 21 days". (3) "The employer may refuse the request only if: (a) the employer has: (i)
-- discussed the request with the employee; and (ii) genuinely tried to reach an agreement ...; and (b) ... have not reached such an
-- agreement; and (c) the employer has had regard to the consequences of the refusal for the employee; and (d) the refusal is on
-- reasonable business grounds." (5) grounds include too costly, no capacity to change others' arrangements, impractical, significant
-- loss in efficiency or productivity, significant negative impact on customer service.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days the employer has to respond` MEANS 21

GIVEN `discussed` IS A BOOLEAN, `genuinely tried to agree` IS A BOOLEAN, `agreement reached` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `had regard to the consequences` IS A BOOLEAN, `reasonable business grounds` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employer may refuse the request` IF
        `discussed`
    AND `genuinely tried to agree`
    AND (NOT `agreement reached`)
    AND `had regard to the consequences`
    AND `reasonable business grounds`

§§ `ss 67, 70, 71, 76, 84 -- unpaid parental leave`

-- s 70: "An employee is entitled to 12 months of unpaid parental leave if: (a) the leave is associated with: (i) the birth of a child
-- of the employee or the employee's spouse or de facto partner; or (ii) the placement of a child ... for adoption; and (b) the
-- employee has or will have a responsibility for the care of the child."
-- s 67(1): an employee other than a casual is not entitled "unless the employee has, or will have, completed at least 12 months of
-- continuous service with the employer immediately before the date that applies"; (2) a casual must be a regular casual for at least
-- 12 months with a reasonable expectation of continuing; (3) the date is the expected date of birth for "birth-related leave starting
-- before the birth", otherwise the day the leave starts.
GIVEN `associated with a birth or adoption` IS A BOOLEAN, `responsible for the care of the child` IS A BOOLEAN, `casual` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `months of continuous service at the relevant date` IS A NUMBER,
      `regular casual for 12 months with an expectation of continuing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employee is entitled to unpaid parental leave` IF
        `associated with a birth or adoption`
    AND `responsible for the care of the child`
    AND (    ((NOT `casual`) AND `months of continuous service at the relevant date` AT LEAST 12)
         OR  (`casual` AND `regular casual for 12 months with an expectation of continuing`))

-- s 70: 12 months. s 76(1): the employee may request an extension "for a further period of up to 12 months"; (7) "not entitled to
-- extend ... beyond 24 months after the date of birth". s 71(3): a pregnant employee's leave may start "up to 6 weeks before the
-- expected date of birth" (or earlier by agreement). s 72A: up to 100 flexible days (or more if prescribed) come out of the 12 months.
-- There is no longer a s 72 in Part 2-2 (no rule limiting the parents' concurrent leave): only s 72A survives.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`months of unpaid parental leave` MEANS 12

GIVEN `months requested beyond the first 12` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the longest period of leave in months after the birth` MEANS
    `the lesser of` (12 PLUS `the lesser of` `months requested beyond the first 12` 12) 24

GIVETH A NUMBER
`weeks before the expected birth leave may start without agreement` MEANS 6

-- s 84: "On ending a period of unpaid parental leave, an employee is entitled to return to: (a) the employee's pre-parental leave
-- position; or (b) if that position no longer exists -- an available position for which the employee is qualified and suited nearest
-- in status and pay".
GIVEN `the pre-leave position still exists` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A STRING
`the position the employee returns to` MEANS
    IF `the pre-leave position still exists`
    THEN "the pre-parental leave position"
    ELSE "an available position nearest in status and pay"

§§ `ss 87, 90, 96 -- annual leave and personal/carer's leave`

-- s 87(1): "For each year of service ... (other than periods of employment as a casual employee ...), an employee is entitled to: (a) 4
-- weeks of paid annual leave; or (b) 5 weeks ... if" an award or agreement describes the employee as a shiftworker (or s 87(3)).
-- (2): the entitlement "accrues progressively during a year of service ... according to the employee's ordinary hours of work".
-- s 96(1): "10 days of paid personal/carer's leave" each year, casuals excluded; (2) accrues progressively in the same way.
-- READING (an inference): the accrued amounts below assume the same ordinary hours in every month of the period.
GIVEN `casual` IS A BOOLEAN, `shiftworker` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`weeks of annual leave per year` MEANS
    IF `casual` THEN 0
    ELSE IF `shiftworker` THEN 5 ELSE 4

GIVEN `casual` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days of personal leave per year` MEANS
    IF `casual` THEN 0 ELSE 10

GIVEN `casual` IS A BOOLEAN, `shiftworker` IS A BOOLEAN, `months of service` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`weeks of annual leave accrued` MEANS
    `weeks of annual leave per year` `casual` `shiftworker` TIMES `months of service` DIVIDED BY 12

GIVEN `casual` IS A BOOLEAN, `months of service` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days of personal leave accrued` MEANS
    `days of personal leave per year` `casual` TIMES `months of service` DIVIDED BY 12

-- s 90(2): "If, when the employment of an employee ends, the employee has a period of untaken paid annual leave, the employer must
-- pay the employee the amount that would have been payable ... had the employee taken that period of leave." (1): that is the "base
-- rate of pay for the employee's ordinary hours of work in the period". s 90(2) applies however the employment ends.
GIVEN `untaken ordinary hours` IS A NUMBER, `base hourly rate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the annual leave paid out on termination` MEANS
    `untaken ordinary hours` TIMES `base hourly rate`

§§ `s 111 -- jury service`

-- s 111(1): applies to an employee absent "because of jury service" who "is not a casual employee". (2) "the employer must pay the
-- employee at the employee's base rate of pay for the employee's ordinary hours of work in the period." (3)-(4) if the employer
-- requires evidence of jury service pay, "the employee is not entitled to payment ... unless the employee provides the evidence",
-- and the payment "is reduced by the total amount of jury service pay". (5) "the employer is only required to pay the employee for the
-- first 10 days of absence". The jury service pay itself is set by State law: an INPUT. The Act does not say what happens if jury pay
-- exceeds the base pay; READING (an inference): the payment does not go below nil.
GIVEN `casual` IS A BOOLEAN, `days absent` IS A NUMBER, `base pay per day` IS A NUMBER, `jury service pay for the first 10 days` IS A NUMBER,
      `evidence required` IS A BOOLEAN, `evidence provided` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the employer's jury service payment` MEANS
    IF `casual` THEN 0
    ELSE IF `evidence required` AND (NOT `evidence provided`) THEN 0
         ELSE IF `evidence required`
              THEN `the greater of` (`the lesser of` `days absent` 10 TIMES `base pay per day` MINUS `jury service pay for the first 10 days`) 0
              ELSE `the lesser of` `days absent` 10 TIMES `base pay per day`

§§ `ss 114-116 -- public holidays`

-- s 115(1)(a): New Year's Day, Australia Day, Good Friday, Easter Monday, Anzac Day, the Sovereign's birthday holiday "on the day on
-- which it is celebrated in a State or Territory", Christmas Day, Boxing Day; "(b) any other day ... declared or prescribed by or
-- under a law of a State or Territory to be observed generally within the State ... as a public holiday". Whether Western Australia
-- Day or Labour Day is declared is a question of the WA Act: an INPUT here.
DECLARE Holiday IS ONE OF
    `New Year's Day`
    `Australia Day`
    `Good Friday`
    `Easter Monday`
    `Anzac Day`
    `the Sovereign's birthday holiday`
    `Christmas Day`
    `Boxing Day`
    `Western Australia Day`
    `Labour Day`
    `Melbourne Cup Day`

GIVEN `day` IS A Holiday, `declared by State law where the employee is based` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the day is a public holiday` IF
    CONSIDER `day`
    WHEN `Western Australia Day` THEN `declared by State law where the employee is based`
    WHEN `Labour Day` THEN `declared by State law where the employee is based`
    WHEN `Melbourne Cup Day` THEN `declared by State law where the employee is based`
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 116: absent on a public holiday, "the employer must pay the employee at the employee's base rate of pay for the employee's
-- ordinary hours of work on the day". Note: no payment if the employee has no ordinary hours that day (a casual not rostered on).
GIVEN `ordinary hours on the day` IS A NUMBER, `base hourly rate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the public holiday payment` MEANS
    `ordinary hours on the day` TIMES `base hourly rate`

§§ `ss 23, 117, 118, 119, 121, 123 -- notice and redundancy`

-- s 117(1)-(2): "An employer must not terminate an employee's employment unless" it gives written notice of at least the minimum
-- period, or payment in lieu. (3) table on "continuous service with the employer at the end of the day the notice is given": not more
-- than 1 year -- 1 week; more than 1 but not more than 3 years -- 2 weeks; more than 3 but not more than 5 -- 3 weeks; more than 5
-- years -- 4 weeks; "(b) then increase the period by 1 week if the employee is over 45 years old and has completed at least 2 years
-- of continuous service". (4) casual service does not count.
-- s 118: "A modern award or enterprise agreement may include terms specifying the period of notice an employee must give". The Act
-- itself sets no notice for an employee who resigns.
-- s 123(1): the Division (notice and redundancy) does not apply to "(a) an employee employed for a specified period of time, for a
-- specified task, or for the duration of a specified season; (b) an employee whose employment is terminated because of serious
-- misconduct; (c) a casual employee".
-- READING (an inference): "over 45 years old" is read as an age greater than 45 (so 45 and a half qualifies, 45 exactly does not).
DECLARE Termination HAS
    `at the employer's initiative` IS A BOOLEAN
    `casual employee` IS A BOOLEAN
    `fixed term, task or season` IS A BOOLEAN
    `serious misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
    `years of continuous service` IS A NUMBER
    `age` IS A NUMBER
    `job no longer required by anyone` IS A BOOLEAN
    `ordinary and customary turnover of labour` IS A BOOLEAN
    `employer insolvent or bankrupt` IS A BOOLEAN
    `employees counted under s 23` IS A NUMBER
    `apprentice` IS A BOOLEAN
    `industry-specific redundancy scheme applies` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `t` IS A Termination
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Division 11 applies` IF
        (NOT `t`'s `casual employee`)
    AND (NOT `t`'s `fixed term, task or season`)
    AND (NOT `t`'s `serious misconduct`)

GIVEN `t` IS A Termination
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act requires the employer to give notice` IF
        `t`'s `at the employer's initiative`
    AND `Division 11 applies` `t`

GIVEN `years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the notice table in weeks` MEANS
    IF `years` AT MOST 1 THEN 1
    ELSE IF `years` AT MOST 3 THEN 2
         ELSE IF `years` AT MOST 5 THEN 3
              ELSE 4

GIVEN `t` IS A Termination
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the minimum period of notice in weeks` MEANS
    IF NOT `the Act requires the employer to give notice` `t` THEN 0
    ELSE IF `t`'s `age` GREATER THAN 45 AND `t`'s `years of continuous service` AT LEAST 2
         THEN `the notice table in weeks` (`t`'s `years of continuous service`) PLUS 1
         ELSE `the notice table in weeks` (`t`'s `years of continuous service`)

-- s 23(1): "a small business employer ... if the employer employs fewer than 15 employees"; (2)(b) a casual is not counted unless a
-- regular casual; (4) the count includes the employee being dismissed and any others also being dismissed.
GIVEN `employees` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employer is a small business employer` IF
    `employees` LESS THAN 15

-- s 119(1): redundancy pay if employment is terminated "(a) at the employer's initiative because the employer no longer requires the
-- job done by the employee to be done by anyone, except where this is due to the ordinary and customary turnover of labour; or (b)
-- because of the insolvency or bankruptcy of the employer." (2) table on continuous service "on termination": at least 1 but less
-- than 2 years -- 4 weeks; 2-3 -- 6; 3-4 -- 7; 4-5 -- 8; 5-6 -- 10; 6-7 -- 11; 7-8 -- 13; 8-9 -- 14; 9-10 -- 16; "At least 10 years
-- -- 12 weeks". (3) casual service does not count.
-- s 121(1): s 119 does not apply if "(a) the employee's period of continuous service ... is less than 12 months; or (b) the employer is
-- a small business employer". s 123(4): Subdivision B does not apply to "(a) an employee who is an apprentice; or (b) an employee to
-- whom an industry-specific redundancy scheme in a modern award applies".
-- s 22(2)(b) and (4): for redundancy pay (unlike notice) periods of unpaid leave do not count toward continuous service. The years
-- given here are assumed to be already net of them.
GIVEN `years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the redundancy pay period in weeks` MEANS
    IF `years` LESS THAN 1 THEN 0
    ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 2 THEN 4
     ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 3 THEN 6
      ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 4 THEN 7
       ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 5 THEN 8
        ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 6 THEN 10
         ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 7 THEN 11
          ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 8 THEN 13
           ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 9 THEN 14
            ELSE IF `years` LESS THAN 10 THEN 16
             ELSE 12

GIVEN `t` IS A Termination
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `redundancy pay is payable` IF
        `Division 11 applies` `t`
    AND (    (    `t`'s `at the employer's initiative`
              AND `t`'s `job no longer required by anyone`
              AND (NOT `t`'s `ordinary and customary turnover of labour`))
         OR  `t`'s `employer insolvent or bankrupt`)
    AND `t`'s `years of continuous service` AT LEAST 1
    AND (NOT `the employer is a small business employer` (`t`'s `employees counted under s 23`))
    AND (NOT `t`'s `apprentice`)
    AND (NOT `t`'s `industry-specific redundancy scheme applies`)

GIVEN `t` IS A Termination
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the weeks of redundancy pay` MEANS
    IF `redundancy pay is payable` `t`
    THEN `the redundancy pay period in weeks` (`t`'s `years of continuous service`)
    ELSE 0

§§ `ss 382, 383, 389 -- unfair dismissal: who is protected, genuine redundancy`

-- s 383: "The minimum employment period is: (a) if the employer is not a small business employer -- 6 months ...; (b) if the employer is
-- a small business employer -- one year". s 382: protected if the employee "has completed a period of employment ... of at least the
-- minimum employment period" and (i) a modern award covers the person, (ii) an enterprise agreement applies, or (iii) annual earnings
-- are "less than the high income threshold" (a figure set by regulation: an INPUT). The Act says nothing about "probation".
GIVEN `employees` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the minimum employment period in months` MEANS
    IF `the employer is a small business employer` `employees` THEN 12 ELSE 6

GIVEN `months employed` IS A NUMBER, `employees` IS A NUMBER, `award covers` IS A BOOLEAN, `agreement applies` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `annual earnings` IS A NUMBER, `high income threshold` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employee is protected from unfair dismissal` IF
        `months employed` AT LEAST `the minimum employment period in months` `employees`
    AND (`award covers` OR `agreement applies` OR `annual earnings` LESS THAN `high income threshold`)

-- s 389(1): genuine redundancy if "(a) the person's employer no longer required the person's job to be performed by anyone because of
-- changes in the operational requirements of the employer's enterprise; and (b) the employer has complied with any obligation in a
-- modern award or enterprise agreement ... to consult about the redundancy." (2) not genuine "if it would have been reasonable in all
-- the circumstances for the person to be redeployed" within the enterprise or an associated entity's.
GIVEN `job no longer required because of operational changes` IS A BOOLEAN, `consultation obligation complied with` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `redeployment would have been reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the dismissal was a genuine redundancy` IF
        `job no longer required because of operational changes`
    AND `consultation obligation complied with`
    AND (NOT `redeployment would have been reasonable`)

§§ `s 530 -- notice to Centrelink`

-- s 530(1): "If an employer decides to dismiss 15 or more employees for reasons of an economic, technological, structural or similar
-- nature ... the employer must give a written notice about the proposed dismissals to ... Centrelink."
GIVEN `number to be dismissed` IS A NUMBER, `economic, technological, structural or similar reasons` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Centrelink must be notified` IF
        `number to be dismissed` AT LEAST 15
    AND `economic, technological, structural or similar reasons`

§§ `s 536 -- pay slips`

-- s 536(1): "An employer must give a pay slip to each of its employees within one working day of paying an amount to the employee in
-- relation to the performance of work." The contents are prescribed by regulation (not encoded).
GIVEN `working days after payment` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the pay slip was given in time` IF
    `working days after payment` AT MOST 1

§§ `ss 45, 544, 545, 548 -- recovering an underpayment`

-- s 45: "A person must not contravene a term of a modern award." (a civil remedy provision). s 46(1): not unless the award applies.
GIVEN `the award applies to the employer` IS A BOOLEAN, `a term of the award was not complied with` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employer contravened s 45` IF
        `the award applies to the employer`
    AND `a term of the award was not complied with`

-- s 544: an application for an order about a contravention of a civil remedy provision may be made "only if the application is made
-- within 6 years after the day on which the contravention occurred". s 545(5): "A court must not make an order ... in relation to an
-- underpayment that relates to a period that is more than 6 years before the proceedings concerned commenced."
GIVEN `years from the contravention to the application` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the application is in time` IF
    `years from the contravention to the application` AT MOST 6

-- s 545(3): an eligible State or Territory court (a magistrates court) "may order an employer to pay an amount ... if ... (a) the
-- employer was required to pay the amount under this Act or a fair work instrument; and (b) the employer has contravened a civil
-- remedy provision by failing to pay the amount." s 548(1)-(1A): a claim in a magistrates court or the Federal Circuit and Family
-- Court (Division 2) for an amount an employer was required to pay is a small claim if the applicant so indicates; (2) "the court may
-- not award more than: (a) $100,000; or (b) if a higher amount is prescribed by the regulations -- that higher amount." (2A) interest
-- does not count. (5) a lawyer only with leave.
GIVEN `amount claimed` IS A NUMBER, `higher amount prescribed by the regulations` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the whole claim fits the small claims limit` IF
    `amount claimed` AT MOST `the greater of` 100000 `higher amount prescribed by the regulations`

GIVEN `required to pay under the Act or a fair work instrument` IS A BOOLEAN, `failed to pay in contravention of a civil remedy provision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a magistrates court may order payment` IF
        `required to pay under the Act or a fair work instrument`
    AND `failed to pay in contravention of a civil remedy provision`
